diagnosis_text:
A complete four-phase diagnostic sweep of the Kath2 topology found no anomaly consistent with the user's report. Phase 1 confirmed that office_pc (192.168.3.2) can reach all sampled internet-lab hosts (128.168.0.x) with 0% packet loss. Direct probes from the three remaining office-LAN devices — enquiry, exam_cell, and tpo — to pc2 (128.168.0.2) and pc3 (128.168.0.3) all returned 0% loss at 1–2 ms round-trip, ruling out a host-specific or subnet-wide connectivity gap.

The control-plane layer (Phase B) shows RIP converged on all three routers: router1 holds a valid R route to 128.168.0.0/16 via router2.eth3 (WAN 20.0.0.0/8), and router2 holds a valid R route back to 192.168.3.0/24 via router1; the complete bidirectional forwarding path is intact. Phase A (infra_sweep + l2_snapshot) found zero ACL/firewall rules, zero static ARP entries, zero duplicate MACs, and all interfaces UP. Phase B tc_snapshot found only default fq_codel qdiscs — no bandwidth throttling, corruption, or delay injection on any interface. Phase D pressure_sweep found no stress tools, CPU pressure, or socket exhaustion on any device.

No flap script, no shaping rule, no ACL, and no routing gap is present. The reported "all morning" timeout symptom is unconfirmed by any current mechanism or historical trace. The network path from the office LAN to the internet lab is fully functional at the time of investigation.

Root cause: No fault — the reported connectivity loss between 192.168.3.x and 128.168.0.x cannot be reproduced or attributed to any configuration or hardware anomaly in the current lab state.

Fix: No corrective action is warranted. If the symptom recurs, capture a simultaneous traceroute and packet count from an office host to a specific internet-lab IP to narrow the window of investigation.
